<h2>The conviction and the interim plea</h2>
<p>The appellant stands convicted of enticing away the informant&rsquo;s minor daughter and of rape — Sections 366A/34 and 376 IPC with Section 4 of the POCSO Act — and sentenced to life imprisonment with a Rs. 50,000 fine on the POCSO count. With his appeal pending before the Division Bench, he sought bail, marshalling the materials that typically anchor such pleas: the victim&rsquo;s pre-trial statement giving her age as 19, a medical estimate of 17-18 years, the claim that she travelled willingly to Mumbai and married him, and his unblemished conduct on bail during trial.</p>

<h2>Why the testimony prevailed</h2>
<p>The Bench anchored its refusal in the victim&rsquo;s deposition as PW-2: the appellant had held himself out as belonging to a religion other than his own, and had physical relations with her on a promise of marriage, resulting in pregnancy. On age, the Court noted the medical position without diluting it: <em>&ldquo;The evidence of the doctor indicates that the age of the victim was opined to be 17-18 years.&rdquo;</em> A band whose midpoint sits below the age of majority does not assist a convict the way it can assist an undertrial — after conviction, the presumption of innocence has yielded to concurrent judicial findings, and the question becomes whether the record discloses something strong enough to justify interim release. The Bench found nothing of the kind: <em>&ldquo;we are not inclined to admit the appellant on bail. His prayer for bail is hereby rejected at this stage.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>Pre-trial bail and post-conviction bail: different gravity</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Before conviction</strong><br>The presumption of innocence operates; contested age evidence and a victim&rsquo;s statement describing a voluntary relationship can support release, since the court takes only a prima facie view of an unproved case.</div>
  <div class="arrow">vs</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>After conviction</strong><br>A trial court has recorded findings on the same material; suspension of sentence requires the appellate court to see an arguable infirmity strong enough to outweigh those findings — a materially higher bar, heavier still where the sentence is life imprisonment.</div>
</div>
<p>The contrast explains why similar fact patterns produce different bail outcomes at different stages. The same age band of 17-18 years that may generate benefit-of-doubt reasoning before trial hardens against the accused once a Special Court has convicted on the full record. Appellate benches also weigh the realistic hearing horizon of the appeal, the portion of sentence served, and the convict&rsquo;s conduct — but none of those displaces the primacy of the trial findings at the interim stage.</p>
<div class="note">The order decides only the interim application; the correctness of the conviction, the age determination and the sentence all remain open in the pending appeal. The victim&rsquo;s identity is protected by law, and this article reflects only the High Court&rsquo;s published order.</div>
